{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["A checklist is an explicit, human-readable artifact, whereas a model's mapping is an opaque, distributed function.","Non-expert panelists can adapt or ignore checklist instructions if they detect adversarial patterns, unlike a rigid algorithm.","A checklist may be deliberately designed as a fast-and-frugal heuristic rather than a causal model, while machine learning models implicitly seek predictive efficiency."],"overturn_conditions":["Checklists are shown to perform well on adversarial items for reasons unrelated to causal completeness (e.g., panelists ignoring the checklist to apply common sense).","It is proven that single-evaluation adversarial robustness does not predict out-of-distribution reliability for human-executed procedures.","Experts are shown to reliably outperform checklists even on structurally aligned in-distribution items due to tacit causal knowledge."],"shared_structure":["A checklist (like a trained model) encodes a mapping from inputs to outputs based on historical data.","Both can achieve high performance on in-distribution items regardless of whether the mapping is causally faithful or merely a surface proxy.","Both are vulnerable to distributional shifts and adversarial perturbations that exploit spurious correlations.","Genuine generalization requires robustness to interventions that break observed correlations."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_002","SCR_001"],"transfer_claims":["Adversarial probes designed to break checklist cues function identically to adversarial examples used to probe model robustness.","High performance on adversarial probes demonstrates that the checklist encodes causal structure, not just surface patterns.","The distinction between robust performance and in-distribution overfitting applies equally to procedural checklists and predictive models."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A single evaluation can only distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance if it includes adversarial probes where the checklist's surface cues and the underlying causal structure diverge. In the absence of such distributional stress-testing, observed high accuracy is ambiguous between genuine causal understanding and epistemic luck (surface proxy alignment).","counterconditions":["The checklist explicitly disclaims causal completeness and operates as a known surface heuristic, making compliance the intended mechanism and the distinction moot.","The adversarial probes lack ecological validity and do not represent plausible distributional shifts, making performance on them uninformative about real-world reliability.","The expert's intuition incorporates real-time, tacit knowledge of distributional shifts that the frozen checklist cannot access, rendering single-evaluation comparisons inherently biased toward the checklist.","The evaluation observes the panel outperforming the expert on adversarial items, but this is due to the aggregation of diverse interpretations (Condorcet effect) rather than checklist fidelity."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"A checklist encodes a frozen procedural theory mapping observable features to outcomes. If this mapping captures true causal structure, compliance transfers to novel, shifted, or adversarial distributions. If the mapping captures only surface correlations present in the training distribution, compliance will fail when surface cues and causal structure diverge. Therefore, a single evaluation can isolate the mechanism by observing performance on items designed to break the surface→outcome link.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9aea81"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","30be11905db881ea7a4e488a8e68f34e4f6b262380bb3b1a31ceb9763a9